15. Scenario-Specific Response Procedures

15.1 Ransomware

Declare and classify the incident based on affected systems and business impact.

Isolate infected systems and restrict suspected lateral movement.

Protect backup infrastructure from further compromise.

Identify the initial access vector and attacker activity.

Preserve evidence and relevant logs.

Reset compromised credentials and remove persistence.

Validate clean backups before restoration.

Restore critical services in priority order and monitor closely.

15.2 Phishing / Credential Compromise

Collect the suspicious message, URL and affected account information.

Disable or secure the compromised account and revoke active sessions.

Reset credentials and enforce appropriate MFA.

Search for additional affected users and related messages.

Check mailbox rules, authentication logs and suspicious forwarding activity.

Block malicious domains or URLs where appropriate.

Determine whether sensitive data was accessed.

15.3 Malware on Endpoint

Isolate the endpoint while preserving evidence.

Identify the malware and affected files/processes.

Review execution, persistence and network connections.

Check whether credentials or sensitive data may have been exposed.

Remove malware or rebuild the endpoint where required.

Reset exposed credentials and validate security controls.

Monitor the user and related systems for additional activity.

15.4 Data Breach

Identify what data was accessed, changed or exfiltrated.

Determine the affected systems, users and time period.

Contain ongoing unauthorized access.

Preserve evidence and maintain an investigation timeline.

Engage legal/privacy teams to assess notification requirements.

Coordinate approved communications with affected parties.

Implement corrective controls and monitor for follow-on abuse.

15.5 DDoS

Confirm the service disruption and identify the affected service.

Activate DDoS protection, CDN/WAF or upstream mitigation as appropriate.

Apply rate limiting and filtering based on verified traffic patterns.

Monitor availability and service performance.

Coordinate with the ISP, cloud provider or DDoS mitigation provider.

Preserve relevant network evidence.

Review resilience and capacity after service restoration.

16. Incident Response Workflow

The following simplified workflow can be used as the operational sequence:

Stage

Primary question

Main output

1. Prepare

Are people, tools and procedures ready?

Contacts, playbooks, backups, monitoring

2. Detect

What security event has occurred?

Alert / incident record

3. Triage

How serious is it?

Severity and scope

4. Analyze

What happened and how?

Timeline, IOCs, root-cause hypothesis

5. Contain

How do we stop further damage?

Isolated systems/accounts

6. Eradicate

How do we remove the cause?

Clean systems, patched weaknesses

7. Recover

How do we safely restore operations?

Validated services

8. Improve

What must change?

Lessons learned and action plan

17. Decision-Making and Escalation Matrix

Condition

Action

Escalate to

Single low-impact endpoint

Contain and investigate

Security lead

Multiple endpoints affected

Activate coordinated response

Incident Response Lead + IT

Privileged account compromised

Immediately secure account and review access

Incident Response Lead + Management

Critical server compromised

Isolate, preserve evidence and assess business impact

Incident Response Lead + Management

Sensitive data potentially exposed

Contain and start breach assessment

Legal/Compliance + Management

Ransomware affecting critical services

Activate major incident response and recovery

Executive Management + full response team

Third-party compromise

Coordinate containment with vendor

Vendor Manager + Legal + Security

18. Post-Incident Activities and Lessons Learned

NIST emphasizes continuous improvement as part of the current incident response model. Lessons from incidents should be analyzed, prioritized and used to improve cybersecurity risk management. [2]

Conduct a post-incident review after stabilization.

Document the root cause and attack path.

Identify which controls worked and which failed or were missing.

Review response timelines and communication effectiveness.

Update detection rules, playbooks and security configurations.

Patch or redesign affected systems.

Provide additional training where human factors contributed.

Track corrective actions to completion.

Share appropriate threat intelligence with relevant parties.

Update the incident response plan based on lessons learned.

19. Incident Response Metrics

Metric

Purpose

Mean Time to Detect (MTTD)

Measures how quickly suspicious activity is detected.

Mean Time to Respond (MTTR)

Measures how quickly the response begins and progresses.

Time to Containment

Measures how quickly attacker activity is limited.

Time to Recovery

Measures how quickly affected services return to trusted operation.

Number of incidents by severity

Shows incident trends and workload.

Repeat incidents

Identifies unresolved root causes.

Playbook exercise completion

Measures readiness.

Backup restoration success

Measures recovery capability.

20. Testing and Maintenance

Review the plan at least annually and after significant incidents or major technology changes.

Conduct tabletop exercises for ransomware, data breach and account compromise scenarios.

Test technical recovery and backup restoration.

Verify emergency contact information.

Check that incident response tools and logging systems are available.

Update severity definitions and escalation thresholds when business requirements change.

Document exercise findings and track improvements.

21. Practical Incident Response Checklist

☐ Incident reported and incident ID created

☐ Incident response lead notified

☐ Severity and scope assessed

☐ Affected accounts/systems identified

☐ Evidence and logs preserved

☐ Immediate containment completed

☐ Initial access vector investigated

☐ Credentials/tokens secured

☐ Vulnerability or root cause addressed

☐ Malware/persistence removed

☐ Legal/compliance assessment completed when required

☐ Backups validated

☐ Critical services restored

☐ Post-recovery monitoring completed

☐ Lessons learned documented

☐ Corrective actions assigned and tracked

22. Conclusion

A well-defined Incident Response Plan enables an organization to respond to cybersecurity incidents in a structured and controlled manner. The plan should not be treated as a document that is used only after an attack. Preparation, monitoring, training, testing, communication and continuous improvement are essential parts of incident response capability.

The proposed plan provides a practical sequence from preparation and detection through analysis, containment, eradication, recovery and lessons learned. It also defines responsibilities, escalation criteria, communication requirements, evidence handling and scenario-specific procedures. By exercising the plan regularly and updating it when technology, threats or business requirements change, an organization can improve its ability to limit damage, restore services and learn from cybersecurity incidents.

